steps.message:
The bug: my E-value regex `E(-?\d+\.?\d*)` requires a digit right after `E`, but the file uses leading-dot values like `E.01926`, `E.7`, `E-.7`. So every text stroke (which uses `E.0xx`) was missed and only the box's `E8.x`/`E4.x` perimeters were captured. Let me fix the regex to `E(-?\d*\.?\d+)` and re-run.

{
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "sed -i \"s/r'(-?\\\\\\\\d+\\\\\\\\.?\\\\\\\\d*)'/r'(-?\\\\\\\\d*\\\\\\\\.?\\\\\\\\d+)'/\" /app/parse3.py; grep -n \"re.search\" /app/parse3.py\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

The per-word OCR is now unambiguous and mutually consistent:
- **w1** → `flag{gc0d3`
- **w2** → `iz`
- **w3** → `ch4LLenGiNg}`

Combined: `flag{gc0d3 iz ch4LLenGiNg}` (leetspeak for "gcode is challenging"). The geometry analysis already proved the two word separators are spaces (near-zero baseline ink in those gaps), so there are no underscores. Writing the answer to `/app/out.txt`.

{
  "analysis": "All evidence converges. Per-word OCR independently yields flag{gc0d3 / iz / ch4LLenGiNg}. Geometric baseline-ink analysis confirmed the two word gaps are spaces (23 and 83 low-band points, far below the underscore threshold seen in real gaps). The full flag is flag{gc0d3 iz ch4LLenGiNg}.",
  "plan": "Write the decoded text to /app/out.txt and verify the contents.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "printf 'flag{gc0d3 iz ch4LLenGiNg}\\n' > /app/out.txt\ncat /app/out.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "task_complete": true
}
